From 199c0d843246c371cabda10326ccd131f3fdf4b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: srymsjackson <jstrazzo22@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:04:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Harden dashboard auth: independent session secret, /login
 rate limit, session revocation

- SESSION_SECRET now signs session cookies, separate from DASHBOARD_KEY.
  Previously both used the same value, so a second DASHBOARD_KEY leak (it
  already leaked once, into Railway logs) would let an attacker forge
  session cookies directly, bypassing /login entirely.

- /login is now rate-limited (5/min per IP, matching /onboarding/create) via
  the existing slowapi limiter. It was the one genuinely brute-forceable
  endpoint left without a cap; failed attempts are also logged.

- Session cookies now carry a version number checked against a live value
  in the database (new AppSetting table). revoke_all_sessions() bumps it,
  invalidating every outstanding session in one write -- useful right after
  rotating DASHBOARD_KEY, or if a device with a live session is lost. Run
  via 'python -m app.auth'. Stored in the DB rather than a local file since
  Railway's app container filesystem isn't guaranteed to survive a
  redeploy, but the configured database is a separate managed service.

Also: moved the rate-limiter test reset fixture from test_rate_limiting.py
into tests/conftest.py as a global autouse fixture, since /login being
rate-limited now means more than one test module logs in via it -- the
limiter is a shared in-process singleton across the whole test session.

Tests: tests/test_auth_hardening.py (7 new tests). Full suite: 78/78
passing. Verified each new test fails in the right place by reverting its
corresponding code change and re-running (rate limit removal ->
test_login_rate_limited_after_5_per_minute fails; version-check removal ->
test_revoke_all_sessions_invalidates_existing_cookie fails).
